# Line endings
#
# Normalise everything to LF in the repository AND check it out as LF on every
# platform. The `eol=lf` is the part that matters: without it, `text=auto` alone
# normalises the index but still hands Windows a CRLF working tree (because
# core.autocrlf defaults to true there), which is what produces VS Code's
# "this file uses LF but Git is configured to convert them to CRLF" warning.
#
# LF is the right default for this repository specifically:
#   - it deploys to Linux (Vercel), and vercel-build.sh is executed by bash,
#     where a CRLF shebang line is a hard failure;
#   - .ipynb files are JSON written by Jupyter with LF;
#   - the Python tooling in tools/ reads and rewrites these files, and mixed
#     endings make every string match in a patch script a coin toss.
#
# Settings in .gitattributes override core.autocrlf, so this fixes the warning
# for every clone without anyone needing to change their local git config.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicit, so intent survives someone editing the line above.
*.py     text eol=lf
*.ipynb  text eol=lf
*.md     text eol=lf
*.qmd    text eol=lf
*.yml    text eol=lf
*.yaml   text eol=lf
*.json   text eol=lf
*.scss   text eol=lf
*.csv    text eol=lf
*.txt    text eol=lf
*.java   text eol=lf

# Shell scripts must be LF or bash will not run them.
*.sh     text eol=lf

# Binary: never touch these.
*.xlsx   binary
*.png    binary
*.jpg    binary
*.jpeg   binary
*.gif    binary
*.pdf    binary
*.ico    binary
*.woff   binary
*.woff2  binary

# Notebooks are JSON, so a textual diff is noisy but readable. Keep it enabled;
# `git diff` on a notebook is better than nothing when reviewing a change.
*.ipynb  diff
